---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/aspe/aspe3280.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Contractual Obligations vs Contingencies — a Key Distinction" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Common Examples" },
        { id: "s5", label: "What This Section Does NOT Require" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Materiality Threshold" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example — Disclosing a Long-Term Purchase Commitment" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example — a Commitment That Does Not Require Disclosure" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Relationship to IFRS's Commitment Disclosures" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">
</div>



<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Disclosing known commitments not yet recognized as liabilities</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 3280 requires DISCLOSURE of CONTRACTUAL obligations EXISTING at the BALANCE sheet DATE — EVEN where THOSE obligations are NOT yet recognized AS a LIABILITY within the FINANCIAL statements THEMSELVES. THIS is a NARROW, purely DISCLOSURE-oriented standard, GENUINELY distinct FROM Section 3290'S own TREATMENT of UNCERTAIN, contingent EVENTS (Section 3).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Known, existing contractual commitments</div><div class="principle">This Section applies to CONTRACTUAL obligations THAT are KNOWN and EXISTING as OF the balance SHEET date — SUCH as long-TERM purchase COMMITMENTS or OTHER non-cancellable CONTRACTUAL arrangements — WHERE the AMOUNT is NOT yet recognized AS a liability BECAUSE the underlying GOODS or SERVICES have NOT yet been RECEIVED.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Contractual Obligations vs Contingencies — a Key Distinction</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Known commitments, not uncertain future events</div><div class="text-supporting">A CONTRACTUAL obligation is a KNOWN, EXISTING commitment UNDER a SIGNED contract — THE obligation ITSELF is NOT uncertain, ONLY its RECOGNITION as a LIABILITY is DEFERRED until PERFORMANCE occurs. THIS stands IN clear CONTRAST to SECTION 3290'S own TREATMENT of CONTINGENCIES — UNCERTAIN future EVENTS whose OUTCOME is NOT yet KNOWN (see the COMPANION poster).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Common Examples</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Long-term commitments not yet reflected in the Balance Sheet</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Long-term, non-cancellable purchase commitments (e.g. take-or-pay supply contracts)</span><span>Future minimum lease payments under operating leases (cross-referenced from Section 3065)</span><span>Long-term service or licensing agreements with fixed future payment obligations</span><span>Construction or capital expenditure commitments not yet incurred</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>What This Section Does NOT Require</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">No liability recognition, and no uncertain contingency disclosure</div><div class="text-supporting">THIS Section does NOT require RECOGNIZING a LIABILITY for THE contractual OBLIGATION itself (THAT occurs, IF at ALL, only WHEN performance UNDER the CONTRACT actually OCCURS, or under OTHER specific ASPE Sections), and DOES NOT address UNCERTAIN, contingent MATTERS, WHICH remain GOVERNED separately BY Section 3290.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Materiality Threshold</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Only significant obligations warrant disclosure</div><div class="text-supporting">CONSISTENT with the GENERAL materiality CONCEPT introduced IN Section 1000, ONLY contractual OBLIGATIONS that ARE genuinely SIGNIFICANT to a READER'S understanding OF the enterprise'S FUTURE cash REQUIREMENTS warrant DISCLOSURE — ROUTINE, immaterial COMMITMENTS (e.g. a SHORT-term office SUPPLY contract) do NOT require SPECIFIC disclosure UNDER this Section.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — Disclosing a Long-Term Purchase Commitment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A known, existing commitment, disclosed but not yet a liability</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A take-or-pay supply agreement</div><div class="wk-body">A manufacturing company signs a 5-year take-or-pay raw materials supply agreement, committing to purchase a minimum of $600,000 of materials per year, regardless of actual usage.</div><div class="wk-answer">The COMPANY discloses the CONTRACTUAL obligation — $600,000 PER year FOR 5 years, TOTALING $3,000,000 — WITHOUT recognizing ANY liability ON the BALANCE Sheet, SINCE no GOODS have YET been RECEIVED under the AGREEMENT.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — a Commitment That Does Not Require Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">An immaterial, routine arrangement</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A short-term office supplies contract</div><div class="wk-body">The same company also has a 6-month office supplies contract committing it to $8,000 of purchases — an immaterial amount relative to its overall $40,000,000 balance sheet.</div><div class="wk-answer">GIVEN the GENUINELY immaterial AMOUNT relative TO the COMPANY'S overall FINANCIAL statements, <strong>NO specific disclosure</strong> is REQUIRED for THIS routine COMMITMENT under Section 3280.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 3280 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Nature of significant contractual obligations existing at the balance sheet date</span><span>Amount and timing of future payments under such obligations</span><span>Any other terms genuinely significant to understanding the enterprise's future cash requirements</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a contractual obligation is genuinely significant enough to warrant disclosure</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Generally minimal — amounts are typically fixed and known under the underlying contract</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS's Commitment Disclosures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASPE Section 3280 vs IFRS</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASPE Section 3280</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Dedicated standalone standard</span><span>YES</span><span>NO — folded into various standards (e.g. IAS 1, IAS 37)</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">SUBSTANTIVELY similar DISCLOSURE objectives, BUT ASPE gives THIS topic its OWN dedicated, STANDALONE Section, CONSISTENT with the MORE granular, TOPIC-by-topic organizational STYLE we'VE seen THROUGHOUT ASPE relative TO IFRS.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where this Section's requirements apply</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Concept</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Contractual obligation disclosure</span><span>Notes only</span><span>No effect on primary financial statement figures</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 3290</span><span>Contingencies — governs uncertain future events, distinct from this Section's known, existing commitments</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 3065</span><span>Leases — supplies the specific future minimum lease payment disclosures also relevant under this Section</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Contractual obligation</div><div class="glossary-def">A known, existing commitment under a signed contract, disclosed even where not yet recognized as a liability.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Take-or-pay contract</div><div class="glossary-def">A purchase agreement obligating the buyer to pay a minimum amount regardless of the quantity actually taken.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.aspe3280 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
